   770	`cmd/go/internal/modload` raises `*gover.TooNewError` immediately after
   771	`modfile.Parse` has returned. An exit status therefore answers a strictly
   772	stronger question than this contract asks, and it cannot be decomposed back into
   773	its parts. A probe that reads only the status reports every representable future
   774	release as outside `Upstream` and fails P1 against its own classifier — and it
   775	does so silently, starting on the day the case corpus first names a version
   776	above the runner's.
   777	
   778	Step 2 MUST therefore be satisfied by an **isolated measurement**, and SHOULD be
   779	corroborated by a **classified command outcome**:
   780	
   781	- *Isolated.* Take the probed toolchain's own `internal/gover` and its own
   782	  `gover.FromToolchain`, from its `GOROOT` and without transcription, build them
   783	  with that same toolchain, and evaluate `gover.IsValid(v)` for a `go`-directive
   784	  value and `v == "default" ∨ FromToolchain(v) ≠ ""` for a `toolchain` name.
   785	  Neither entry point consults the local version, so this measures
   786	  representability and nothing else. The probe MUST record the path and SHA-256
   787	  of each source it lifts, and MUST refuse to run if the lifted `FromToolchain`
   788	  is no longer self-contained, so a future upstream refactor fails the probe
   789	  instead of being quietly worked around.
   790	- *Corroborating.* Run the real command and classify its outcome into **three**
   791	  states — accepted, too-new, rejected — where too-new counts as representable.
   792	  Too-new MUST be recognised structurally rather than by a loose message match:
   793	  upstream renders `TooNewError` as
   794	  `"%v requires go >= %v (running go %v%v)"`, and the probe MUST require the
   795	  echoed version to equal the value under test, which the command can only
   796	  produce by having parsed and compared it. For the `toolchain` position the
   797	  command form is `GOTOOLCHAIN=local+path`, under which a representable name is
   798	  searched for in `PATH` — "cannot find X in `PATH`" is the accepting outcome —
   799	  and an unrepresentable one is refused before any search with
   800	  `invalid toolchain` or `invalid GOTOOLCHAIN`.
   801	- The two MUST agree wherever the shape layer accepts the value, and a
   802	  disagreement MUST fail the probe. Where the shape layer rejects the value the
   803	  command is measuring the conjunction rather than the semantic layer, and the
   804	  comparison is not applicable.
   805	
   806	The probe run recorded for this contract covers 16 `go`-directive and 13
   807	`toolchain`-directive values on Go 1.25.1 and Go 1.25.5 — 58 measured cases —
   808	with zero divergences and zero isolated-versus-command disagreements. Three
   809	measurements carry the design:
   810	
   811	- both toolchains accept `go 1.23.4rc1` at the shape layer, and the command then
   812	  aborts with `panic: go: internal error: missing go root module`, while the
   813	  isolated `gover.Parse` returns the zero version. That is the concrete failure
   814	  P1 exists to prevent, and it is why the `go`-directive classifier is defined
   815	  over the conjunction rather than over `GoVersionRE` alone;
   816	- both toolchains represent `go 1.99.0`, `go 1.26.0` and `go 1.99rc1` — isolated
   817	  verdict valid, command outcome too-new — so all three are inside `Upstream`
   818	  and stay class 2 and class 3 comparisons. The exit-code-only classifier scored
   819	  them as rejected;
   820	- both toolchains represent `toolchain go1.99.0-custom`, which this contract
   821	  nevertheless classifies `forbidden`; that measurement is what makes
   822	  `C = Upstream` unsatisfiable rather than merely unproven.
   823	
   824	Three regression controls MUST remain runnable from the probe itself rather than
   825	from a hand-edited copy of it, and each MUST fail for one named reason:
   650	- **State the Go alignment as one equality, `compared` equals what upstream
   651	  accepts.** Rejected: it is unsatisfiable, not merely strict. Upstream accepts
   652	  custom distribution names such as `go1.21.0-custom`, and this decision
   653	  classifies exactly those as package influence, so the two sets differ by
   654	  construction and no implementation could satisfy the statement. Splitting it
   655	  into P1 (`C ⊆ Upstream`) and P2 (`Upstream \ F ⊆ C`) keeps both halves exact
   656	  and makes the security subtraction explicit instead of a contradiction.
   657	- **Prove P1 and P2 with fixture vectors only.** Rejected: a fixture's upstream
   658	  column records what this decision believes upstream does, so a shared
   659	  misreading of upstream produces a green suite — which is how the
   660	  `GoVersionRE`-only classifier survived a review cycle. Both layers are
   661	  upstream artifacts that move independently, so the properties are measured by
   662	  an executable probe against real toolchains, and the fixtures assert the
   663	  partition the probe validates.
   664	- **Measure the semantic layer with a command's exit status** (`go mod tidy`
   665	  exits 0 ⇔ upstream can represent the value). Rejected: it is not the semantic
   666	  layer, it is the semantic layer conjoined with the host-version gate, and the
   667	  probe cannot tell the two apart from a status. It scores every representable
   668	  release above the runner's own — `go 1.99.0`, `go 1.26.0`, `go 1.99rc1` — as
   669	  outside `Upstream`, so the probe fails P1 against classes this decision
   670	  deliberately keeps as comparisons. The failure mode is the dangerous kind: the
   671	  probe is green until the corpus first names a version above the runner, then
   672	  goes red for a defect that is entirely in the probe, and it goes red on the
   673	  release gate rather than in review. Rejected alongside it: **matching the
   674	  too-new message loosely**, since a substring of an error string is not a
   675	  measurement of anything; the retained corroborating check requires the version
   676	  the command echoes back to equal the value under test, which upstream can only
   677	  produce by having represented it.
   678	- **Drop the command measurement once the isolated one exists.** Rejected: the
   679	  isolated harness is upstream's code but not upstream's composition, so on its
   680	  own it could be wired up wrongly and still look self-consistent. Keeping both
   681	  and requiring them to agree wherever the shape layer accepts the value costs
   682	  one comparison per case and catches exactly that class of error. The
   683	  disagreement itself is the check; neither measurement is authoritative alone.
   684	- **Keep the superseded classifications only in review history.** Rejected: each
   685	  one produced a green probe, and a property that was silently dropped is
   686	  indistinguishable from one that never existed. The three superseded
   687	  classifications stay runnable from the probe binary as controls that are
   688	  required to fail, so a change that reintroduces one is caught by a control that
   689	  stops failing.
